    {
     "head": "Random movement",
     "group": "random",
     "text": "Molecules and ions are always moving. Each particle moves in one direction until it hits another particle or a wall, and then it moves in a new direction. Nothing guides it. This is _random movement_."
    },
    {
     "head": "Concentration",
     "group": "conc",
     "text": "The number of particles in a fixed volume is the _concentration_. To compare two concentrations, compare equal volumes: the volume with more particles has the higher concentration. In the pictures, a higher concentration is red and a lower concentration is blue."
    },
    {
     "head": "A concentration gradient",
     "group": "gradient",
     "text": "A difference in concentration between two regions is a _concentration gradient_. On a graph of the concentration, the gradient is a slope, and a larger difference makes a steeper slope. Particles go “down” the gradient when they go from the higher concentration to the lower concentration."
    },
    {
     "head": "What “net” means",
     "text": "Particles cross between two regions in both directions. More particles cross from the higher concentration to the lower concentration, because more particles are there. The _net movement_ is the number crossing one way minus the number crossing the other way."
    },
    {
     "head": "Diffusion",
     "group": "net",
     "text": "_Diffusion_ is the _net movement_ of particles from a region of their higher concentration to a region of their lower concentration (i.e. _down a concentration gradient_), as a result of their random movement."
    },
    {
     "head": "Equilibrium",
     "group": "equal",
     "text": "When the two concentrations are equal, the net movement is zero. This is _equilibrium_. The particles do not stop: they still move, and equal numbers cross each way. In the body, equilibrium is usually not reached, because breathing and blood flow maintain the concentration gradient."
    },
    {
     "head": "The energy for diffusion",
     "group": "energy",
     "text": "The energy for diffusion comes from the _kinetic energy_ of random movement of molecules and ions. Diffusion does not use energy from respiration (the chemical reactions in cells that release energy)."
    }
