    {
     "head": "Water in digestion",
     "group": "digestion",
     "text": "Digestive enzymes are dissolved in water. They make large food molecules, such as starch and fat, into small, soluble molecules. These small molecules dissolve, and are absorbed from the gut in solution."
    },
    {
     "head": "Water in excretion",
     "group": "excretion",
     "text": "Excretion is the removal of the waste products of metabolism (the chemical reactions in cells) and substances in excess of requirements. Water is the solvent for these substances: urea leaves the body dissolved in the water of urine."
    },
    {
     "head": "Water in transport",
     "group": "transport",
     "text": "Substances are carried dissolved in water. Blood plasma carries glucose, ions and urea in solution. Xylem carries water and mineral ions, and phloem carries sucrose and amino acids."
    }
